WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:06.509
OK, in this section, let's take a look at the last pillar of the collaboration production

2
00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:12.070
pipeline and see what additional collaboration tools are being implemented for our project

3
00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.650
workflows.

4
00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:19.469
So we already know that the Epic Games version control solution utilizes Perforce.

5
00:00:19.469 --> 00:00:24.070
From there, Epic Games asset tracking is managed through ShotGrid.

6
00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:26.829
But what is our third collaborative production pillar?

7
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:35.109
Well, Epic Games teams use a mix of video conference tools, for example, Zoom, along with asset

8
00:00:35.109 --> 00:00:41.149
review tools like SyncSketch, alongside other internal asset review tools for interactive

9
00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:44.439
collaboration during a production.

10
00:00:44.439 --> 00:00:46.439
So what is SyncSketch?

11
00:00:46.439 --> 00:00:51.750
Well, SyncSketch is a visual communication tool that allows remote teams to review

12
00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:55.179
and annotate media in real time.

13
00:00:55.179 --> 00:01:00.619
SyncSketch also offers integrations with a variety of other DCC applications, such as

14
00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:07.900
Autodesk Maya and ShotGrid, along with generic Python API access for media import automation.

15
00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:13.819
Epic Games utilizes this integration with ShotGrid specifically to access ShotGrid playlists

16
00:01:13.819 --> 00:01:17.379
and synchronized notes and sketches on the backend.

17
00:01:17.379 --> 00:01:21.340
Alright, that finishes our collaboration production pipeline.

18
00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.099
In our next section, let's begin wrapping up this course with some final recommendations.
